(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antifungals-quiz"]={"title":"Antifungals Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antifungal works by inhibiting microtubules and so stopping cell division?","choices":["Griseofulvin","Flucytosine","Caspofungin","Terbinafine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is the agent the syllabus calls a mitotic inhibitor. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What is the echinocandin mechanism of action?","choices":["Inhibition of lanosterol demethylase, progressively reducing ergosterol in the fungal membrane","Formation of membrane channels allowing potassium to leak from the cell","Disruption of the mitotic spindle, halting cell division","Inhibition of 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase, lowering formation of 1,3-beta-D-glucan in the cell wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin are the three agents, and the wall target is unique among the antifungals here. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which indications does the lecture give for the echinocandins?","choices":["Zygomycete infection such as Mucor","Oesophageal candidiasis, systemic aspergillosis not responding to itraconazole or amphotericin B, and febrile neutropenic patients not responding to antibiotics","Cryptococcal meningitis, oral and vaginal candidiasis, and prophylaxis in advanced human immunodeficiency virus infection, together with urinary candidiasis","Dermatophyte infection of the nails and scalp"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — largely a salvage and neutropenic-fever role rather than first-line therapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which azoles are noted as teratogenic?","choices":["Ketoconazole and posaconazole only","None of the azoles carries a teratogenicity caution","Only the topical imidazole derivatives","Itraconazole, and fluconazole and voriconazole in animals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reproductive safety is a recurring caution across the azole group. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which three agents make up the echinocandin class?","choices":["Naftifine, terbinafine and tolnaftate","Fluconazole, voriconazole and posaconazole","Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin","Amphotericin B, nystatin and natamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Azoles and allylamines both reduce ergosterol. At which enzyme does each act?","choices":["Both inhibit lanosterol demethylase, differing only in binding site","Azoles inhibit lanosterol demethylase; allylamines inhibit squalene epoxidase","Azoles inhibit squalene epoxidase; allylamines inhibit lanosterol demethylase","Azoles inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase; allylamines inhibit lanosterol demethylase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same pathway, different steps — which is why both end in decreased ergosterol. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which mycoses does the lecture place in the subcutaneous category?","choices":["Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis","Tinea pedis, tinea cruris and tinea corporis","Aspergillosis, cryptococcosis and histoplasmosis","Oral and vaginal candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the middle tier, affecting skin layers rather than the surface or the internal organs. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A patient develops oral candidiasis after a course of broad-spectrum antibiotics. Which predisposing factor does this illustrate?","choices":["Suppression of competing organisms","Loss of barriers","Metabolic abnormality","A warm and moist local environment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Removing the bacterial flora leaves the niche open, which is why antibiotic exposure is itself a fungal risk factor. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which antifungal acts on nucleic acid synthesis rather than on the membrane or wall?","choices":["Flucytosine, through conversion to a fluorinated nucleotide that inhibits thymidylate synthase","Caspofungin, by inhibiting glucan synthesis","Terbinafine, by inhibiting squalene epoxidase early in the fungal ergosterol synthesis pathway","Nystatin, by forming membrane channels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It and griseofulvin are the two agents outside the membrane-and-wall scheme. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Why is ketoconazole rarely used now?","choices":["It is available only intravenously and causes severe infusion reactions requiring routine premedication before every dose","It is the only azole ineffective against Candida","It causes visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients","It does not enter the central nervous system and it inhibits cytochrome P450 3A4, producing significant drug interactions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and itraconazole with its wider spectrum largely replaced it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Voriconazole is derived from which other azole?","choices":["Itraconazole","Ketoconazole","Clotrimazole","Fluconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a fluconazole derivative introduced in 2002, which then took over systemic aspergillosis from amphotericin B. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Why does the fungal cell wall present a target that the bacterial cell wall does not?","choices":["It has no wall, so only membrane-active agents work","It contains chitin and glucans rather than peptidoglycan, so agents such as the echinocandins are needed","It contains peptidoglycan in a considerably thicker layer, so higher beta-lactam doses are required","It contains ergosterol, which the echinocandins bind"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams and vancomycin act on peptidoglycan, which fungi do not have — which is why antibacterials are useless against them. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which of these is a topical imidazole derivative rather than a systemic azole?","choices":["Itraconazole","Clotrimazole","Posaconazole","Voriconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The topical group also includes econazole, miconazole, butoconazole, tioconazole, oxiconazole, sulconazole and sertaconazole. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"From what source is amphotericin B derived?","choices":["A soil Streptomycetaceae organism","Penicillium griseofulvum","Bacillus polymyxa","The soil bacterium Amycolatopsis orientalis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which places it alongside several antibacterials in being a natural product of a soil microbe. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What advantage do the lipid formulations of amphotericin B offer, and at what cost?","choices":["Improved oral bioavailability, at a modest increase in cost","A broader spectrum covering the Zygomycetes, at greater renal risk","A shorter infusion time, at the expense of efficacy","Reduced toxicity, at 20 to 50 times the cost of the deoxycholate formulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The three named lipid products are the lipid complex, the colloidal suspension and the liposomal form. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which organisms is amphotericin B indicated against?","choices":["Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Candida, Coccidioides and Aspergillus, the last reserved for invasive infection","Dermatophyte infections of the skin, hair and nails only, with no activity against any of the systemic fungal organisms","Helminths including hookworm and pinworm","Influenza A and B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its breadth is why it remained the reference agent until voriconazole displaced it for aspergillosis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A patient with poorly controlled diabetes develops a fungal infection. Which predisposing category does this represent?","choices":["Suppression of competing organisms","Metabolic abnormality","Immunodeficiency","Loss of barriers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — diabetes is the lecture's own example for that category. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How does pyrantel pamoate kill its target parasites?","choices":["It inhibits squalene epoxidase in the parasite, progressively depleting the membrane of ergosterol until it loses integrity","It blocks neuraminidase and prevents the parasite from leaving host cells","It releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, acting as a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker that causes paralysis and death","It inhibits formation of helminth microtubules and blocks glucose uptake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is used for pinworm and hookworm. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Which amphotericin B formulation is the conventional, non-lipid one?","choices":["Amphotericin B colloidal suspension, marketed as Amphotec","Amphotericin B deoxycholate, marketed as Fungizone","Amphotericin B lipid complex, marketed as Abelcet","Liposomal amphotericin B, marketed as AmBisome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The three lipid alternatives are the lipid complex, the colloidal suspension and the liposomal form. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How long does terbinafine treatment take for fingernail and toenail infection?","choices":["A single dose for both sites","Six to twelve weeks for fingernails and up to twelve months for toenails","One week for fingernails and one month for toenails","Six to nine months for fingernails and up to twelve months for toenails"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As with griseofulvin, the duration follows nail growth, which is the counselling point that keeps patients on therapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A patient develops athlete's foot after prolonged use of occlusive footwear. Which predisposing factor is this?","choices":["A warm moist environment","Loss of barriers","Immunodeficiency","Metabolic abnormality"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture's own examples for that category are diaper rash and athlete's foot. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which electrolyte and renal effects accompany amphotericin B?","choices":["Isolated hepatotoxicity with no renal involvement","Bone marrow suppression as the dose-limiting toxicity","Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia, hypotension, uraemia in about 80 percent with decreased filtration, and renal tubule damage mitigated by hydration with normal saline","Hyperkalaemia and hypercalcaemia with a rising glomerular filtration rate, hypertension, and tubular hypertrophy that resolves without any need for hydration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The saline hydration is the practical countermeasure worth remembering. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What is the allylamine mechanism of action?","choices":["Binding ergosterol already present and forming membrane channels","Inhibition of glucan synthesis in the cell wall","Inhibition of squalene epoxidase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","Inhibition of lanosterol demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Same end point as the azoles, reached at an earlier step of the pathway. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"From which organism is griseofulvin derived?","choices":["Streptomycetaceae in soil","Bacillus polymyxa","Streptomyces griseus","Penicillium griseofulvum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a mould product, like several of the antibacterials in this lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which azole has the best oral absorption, and how is it eliminated?","choices":["Itraconazole, excreted unchanged in the faeces","Posaconazole, excreted by the lungs","Fluconazole, excreted in the urine","Ketoconazole, excreted in the bile"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and its urinary excretion is part of why a single dose treats vaginal candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"An immunocompromised patient has systemic aspergillosis that has not responded to itraconazole or amphotericin B. Which class does the lecture position for this?","choices":["Echinocandins","Polyenes","Allylamines","Mitotic inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — their listed role is largely salvage, in refractory aspergillosis, oesophageal candidiasis, and febrile neutropenia not responding to antibiotics. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"How long does griseofulvin treatment take for scalp, fingernail and toenail infection?","choices":["A single dose for the scalp, with topical therapy thereafter for nails","About one month for the scalp, six to nine months for fingernails, and up to twelve months for toenails","Approximately one week for the scalp, one month for the fingernails, and three months for the toenails","Three months for all three sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The gradient follows how slowly each keratin structure grows out, and it is the counselling point that keeps patients adherent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which azole replaced amphotericin B for systemic aspergillosis, and what is its characteristic adverse effect?","choices":["Fluconazole, with hepatotoxicity","Itraconazole, with severe and often irreversible peripheral neuropathy","Voriconazole, with effects on vision in about 30 percent of patients","Posaconazole, with QT prolongation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It also affects liver and renal function, and its intravenous vehicle, cyclodextrin, accumulates in renal failure. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which enzymes does voriconazole interact with?","choices":["Cytochrome P450 1A2 and 2C9 only, by induction","Cytochrome P450 3A4 only","No cytochrome P450 enzymes; it is renally cleared unchanged","Cytochrome P450 2C19, 2C9 and 3A4"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a broader set than the other azoles, which is part of why it needs care in combination. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which adverse effects and interactions accompany griseofulvin?","choices":["Nephrotoxicity and electrolyte wasting","Visual disturbance in 30 percent with hepatic and renal effects","Headache in about 15 percent, mental confusion, fatigue and blurred vision, with induction of cytochrome P450 1A2 and 2C9","Inhibition of cytochrome P450 3A4, producing raised plasma levels of co-administered substrate drugs and a risk of toxicity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note it induces enzymes where the azoles inhibit them — opposite directions of interaction within the same broad category of drug. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"}]};
